WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.661
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 07 of the

2
00:00:03.661 --> 00:00:07.813
Federal Modernization
Workbook, PKI and Certificates.

3
00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:10.776
It's for the PKI engineers
and architects who will

4
00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:14.174
run the move off Active
Directory Certificate Services.

5
00:00:14.174 --> 00:00:17.248
It comes in two parts of about
twenty-five minutes each,

6
00:00:17.248 --> 00:00:19.638
and four modules you can watch separately.

7
00:00:19.638 --> 00:00:21.811
Part 1 is hardening and the target:

8
00:00:21.811 --> 00:00:26.238
first how to make the AD CS that stays
the best-defended system you own,

9
00:00:26.238 --> 00:00:30.975
then the target architecture of four
issuers and one lifecycle platform.

10
00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:36.016
Part 2 is issuing, migrating and
retiring: the Intune certificate profiles,

11
00:00:36.016 --> 00:00:41.383
strong mapping and Entra certificate-based
authentication, then coexistence,

12
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:47.075
the six-wave migration, lifecycle monitoring
and the decommissioning of the old CAs.

13
00:00:47.075 --> 00:00:50.925
The slides show tables,
checklists and decision matrices.

14
00:00:50.925 --> 00:00:53.772
The commands live in the
chapters and appendices,

15
00:00:53.772 --> 00:00:58.008
and the book marks them as illustrative
and untested against production;

16
00:00:58.008 --> 00:01:01.280
I'll point you to them rather
than put them on screen.

17
00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.218
Why this matters.

18
00:01:03.218 --> 00:01:07.280
A certificate is a signed naming claim
that has already been handed out.

19
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.775
It sits in device stores, on smart cards,

20
00:01:09.775 --> 00:01:14.218
in load balancers and in trust
stores nobody remembers configuring.

21
00:01:14.218 --> 00:01:16.618
It doesn't move when the directory moves.

22
00:01:16.618 --> 00:01:20.992
It keeps working until it expires,
and then whatever relied on it stops.

23
00:01:20.992 --> 00:01:25.755
Three properties make AD CS the hardest
part of Active Directory to leave.

24
00:01:25.755 --> 00:01:30.955
An enterprise CA is wired into the forest:
its templates, enrollment services,

25
00:01:30.955 --> 00:01:35.993
NTAuth trust and publication points
live in the configuration partition.

26
00:01:35.993 --> 00:01:40.864
The cloud replacements cover only
part of the estate: Cloud PKI issues

27
00:01:40.864 --> 00:01:45.568
to Intune-managed devices, and Intune
doesn't manage Windows Server.

28
00:01:45.568 --> 00:01:50.467
And domain controllers need certificates
for as long as domain controllers exist.

29
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:54.279
Meanwhile the rules are moving
under certificates already issued.

30
00:01:54.279 --> 00:01:59.072
Public TLS maximum validity
is two hundred days today,

31
00:01:59.072 --> 00:02:05.355
a hundred days from March 15, 2027, and
forty-seven days from March 15, 2029.

32
00:02:05.355 --> 00:02:11.926
KB5014754 put domain controllers in
Full Enforcement on February 11,

33
00:02:11.926 --> 00:02:18.043
2025, and Compatibility mode has
been gone since September 9, 2025.

34
00:02:18.043 --> 00:02:25.795
NIST's draft post-quantum transition plan
proposes deprecating RSA-2048 signatures

35
00:02:25.795 --> 00:02:33.220
after 2030 and disallowing RSA and ECDSA
signatures after 2035, and OMB M-26-15 gave

36
00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:40.317
agencies a hundred and twenty days from
June 24, 2026 to submit a migration plan.

37
00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:43.618
So the move off AD CS
runs as its own program.

38
00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:45.950
It fails at expiry, not at cutover,

39
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:49.680
which can be months later
and far from its cause.

40
00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.280
What AD CS holds up.

41
00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.643
It isn't one server.

42
00:02:53.643 --> 00:02:56.451
The typical estate is
a two-tier hierarchy:

43
00:02:56.451 --> 00:03:01.025
an offline standalone root that signs
the issuing CAs and its own CRL,

44
00:03:01.025 --> 00:03:06.481
and enterprise issuing CAs that issue from
templates stored in Active Directory.

45
00:03:06.481 --> 00:03:12.098
Every enterprise CA also lives as objects
in the forest's Public Key Services

46
00:03:12.098 --> 00:03:18.330
branch: certificate templates, enrollment
services, the NTAuth certificates object,

47
00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:22.105
AIA and CDP containers, key
recovery agents and OIDs.

48
00:03:22.105 --> 00:03:27.980
Those objects are forest-wide, which is
exactly what ties AD CS to Active Directory.

49
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:33.049
Domain members enroll through Group Policy
autoenrollment, which runs every eight

50
00:03:33.049 --> 00:03:37.755
hours and renews a certificate after it
passes eighty percent of its validity.

51
00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:41.200
Chapter 02's inventory
has four layers: the CAs,

52
00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.005
the templates, the issued
certificates and the relying parties.

53
00:03:46.005 --> 00:03:49.419
The relying-party layer decides
the order of work later,

54
00:03:49.419 --> 00:03:54.317
because a new issuer is useful only once
everything that validates it trusts it.

55
00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:59.218
Then every template and use case gets
one of four verdicts, asked in order.

56
00:03:59.218 --> 00:04:02.168
RETIRE first, if the
consumer is going away.

57
00:04:02.168 --> 00:04:07.497
MIGRATE, if Cloud PKI through
Intune or Entra CBA replaces it:

58
00:04:07.497 --> 00:04:11.655
Wi-Fi, VPN, device
authentication, tenant sign-in.

59
00:04:11.655 --> 00:04:16.731
BRIDGE, if it depends on AD itself
or a server the cloud can't reach:

60
00:04:16.731 --> 00:04:20.467
domain controllers, LDAPS,
NPS and NTAuth trust.

61
00:04:20.467 --> 00:04:23.964
RETAIN is what's left, such
as HSM-held code signing,

62
00:04:23.964 --> 00:04:27.205
and every entry gets an
owner and a review date.

63
00:04:27.205 --> 00:04:30.836
Appendix B applies these
verdicts template by template;

64
00:04:30.836 --> 00:04:33.140
we'll come back to it in Module 4.

65
00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.440
Module 1: hardening what stays.

66
00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:41.802
A migration off AD CS takes months, and
for some workloads it never finishes.

67
00:04:41.802 --> 00:04:47.293
Throughout that time the enterprise CA
keeps issuing certificates that domain

68
00:04:47.293 --> 00:04:52.865
controllers, VPN gateways, RADIUS servers
and Entra ID accept as proof of identity.

69
00:04:52.865 --> 00:04:57.782
A CA can create credentials the whole
environment trusts, so one over-privileged

70
00:04:57.782 --> 00:05:02.553
account or misconfigured setting on it
can undo every other identity control.

71
00:05:02.553 --> 00:05:07.390
That makes hardening the first technical
workstream, not a cleanup task at the end.

72
00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:10.059
Everything in this module is defensive:

73
00:05:10.059 --> 00:05:14.630
what each misconfiguration is, how
to detect it, and how to fix it.

74
00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:20.823
The program carries one rule forward
unchanged: misconfigurations rated Critical

75
00:05:20.823 --> 00:05:25.955
or High are remediated and verified
before any certificate workload migrates.

76
00:05:25.955 --> 00:05:30.060
Migrating from a CA that can be
made to issue arbitrary credentials

77
00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.092
propagates the weakness into
whatever trusts the hierarchy next.

78
00:05:34.092 --> 00:05:38.360
And under the bring-your-own-CA
model the program recommends,

79
00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:43.055
the new Cloud PKI issuing CA chains
to the existing AD CS hierarchy,

80
00:05:43.055 --> 00:05:46.555
so it inherits every weakness
the day it goes live.

81
00:05:46.555 --> 00:05:51.730
The ESC names come from SpecterOps' 2021
research; Microsoft adopted the same

82
00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:56.743
labels in Defender for Identity, which
is why they're the shared vocabulary.

83
00:05:56.743 --> 00:06:00.043
The book uses them only as
labels for defensive checks.

84
00:06:00.043 --> 00:06:02.730
The classes live in four control points.

85
00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:06.442
Template objects hold ESC1 through ESC4.

86
00:06:06.442 --> 00:06:12.650
The Public Key Services objects hold
ESC5, which Defender for Identity doesn't

87
00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:18.954
assess, so detection is an ACL review plus
directory change auditing, event 5136.

88
00:06:18.954 --> 00:06:23.356
The CA's own configuration
holds ESC6 and ESC7,

89
00:06:23.356 --> 00:06:27.293
and HTTP enrollment endpoints hold ESC8.

90
00:06:27.293 --> 00:06:32.524
Certificate template assessments are
available to every customer with AD CS;

91
00:06:32.524 --> 00:06:37.191
CA assessments need a Defender for
Identity sensor on the AD CS server,

92
00:06:37.191 --> 00:06:40.330
so install it on every
enterprise CA that stays.

93
00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:42.030
Two caveats from the book.

94
00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:47.676
In GCC, Defender for Identity uses the
security dot microsoft dot com portal;

95
00:06:47.676 --> 00:06:53.143
Microsoft says new features reach GCC,
GCC High and DoD within ninety days

96
00:06:53.143 --> 00:06:58.430
of commercial release, but preview
features may not be supported there.

97
00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:02.207
The ESC1 and ESC6 assessments
are still labeled preview,

98
00:07:02.207 --> 00:07:07.780
so confirm they appear in your tenant,
and run the manual checks in the meantime.

99
00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:10.880
The template classes, as a checklist.

100
00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:16.154
ESC1: a template lets the requester supply
the subject or subject alternative name,

101
00:07:16.154 --> 00:07:20.893
permits client authentication, and
broad unprivileged groups can enroll,

102
00:07:20.893 --> 00:07:24.868
so a member could obtain a
certificate that names someone else.

103
00:07:24.868 --> 00:07:28.110
Detect it with Defender
for Identity, and by hand:

104
00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:31.111
Supply in the request on
the Subject Name tab,

105
00:07:31.111 --> 00:07:36.219
an authentication purpose such as Client
Authentication, Smart Card Logon,

106
00:07:36.219 --> 00:07:40.598
PKINIT or Any Purpose, and Enroll
granted to Authenticated Users,

107
00:07:40.598 --> 00:07:43.517
Domain Users, Domain
Computers or Everyone.

108
00:07:43.517 --> 00:07:47.804
On the CA, events 4886
and 4887 show requests

109
00:07:47.804 --> 00:07:52.805
where the requester and
the issued subject differ.

110
00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:57.578
Any one remediation closes it: build
the subject from Active Directory,

111
00:07:57.578 --> 00:08:02.916
remove authentication EKUs the template
doesn't need, restrict Enroll, require

112
00:08:02.916 --> 00:08:08.255
CA certificate manager approval, or take
the template off every CA if it's unused.

113
00:08:08.255 --> 00:08:13.605
ESC2 is Any Purpose or no EKU on
a broadly enrollable template.

114
00:08:13.605 --> 00:08:17.129
A no-EKU template restricted
to PKI administrators,

115
00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:20.980
such as a subordinate CA
template, isn't the same exposure.

116
00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:25.230
ESC3 is a broadly enrollable
enrollment agent template.

117
00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:29.542
Treat an enrollment agent certificate
as an impersonation capability.

118
00:08:29.542 --> 00:08:34.336
Microsoft doesn't publish a Windows
Server 2025 procedure for enrollment

119
00:08:34.336 --> 00:08:39.130
agent restrictions, so validate the
existing configuration in a lab first.

120
00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:42.641
ESC4 is write rights or
ownership on a template

121
00:08:42.641 --> 00:08:45.792
held by anyone but the PKI administrators.

122
00:08:45.792 --> 00:08:49.855
Changes are logged as event
5136 only when directory

123
00:08:49.855 --> 00:08:54.380
change auditing is on and the
objects carry a matching SACL.

124
00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:59.684
One trap when you fix it: if you remove
Authenticated Users from a template ACL,

125
00:08:59.684 --> 00:09:02.833
grant each issuing CA's
computer account Read,

126
00:09:02.833 --> 00:09:05.920
or the CA can no longer read the template.

127
00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.032
The directory and CA classes.

128
00:09:09.032 --> 00:09:14.933
ESC5 is unprivileged write access on the
forest's Public Key Services objects.

129
00:09:14.933 --> 00:09:19.824
The NTAuth certificates object holds the
CAs trusted to issue logon and domain

130
00:09:19.824 --> 00:09:23.307
controller certificates,
and each enrollment services

131
00:09:23.307 --> 00:09:26.494
object records what a CA can
issue and who can enroll.

132
00:09:26.494 --> 00:09:29.908
Write access to either is
control over authentication.

133
00:09:29.908 --> 00:09:35.650
Defender for Identity has no ESC5
assessment, so review the ACLs, compare the

134
00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:41.483
NTAuth store with your approved CA list,
and audit changes through event 5136.

135
00:09:41.483 --> 00:09:45.045
ESC6 is the CA-wide flag
that lets any requester

136
00:09:45.045 --> 00:09:48.882
supply subject alternative
names on any template.

137
00:09:48.882 --> 00:09:53.252
Find out why it was turned on, since
some old workflows depended on it,

138
00:09:53.252 --> 00:09:55.670
then clear it and restart the service.

139
00:09:55.670 --> 00:10:00.971
The book corrects a common belief here:
KB5014754 introduced strong mapping,

140
00:10:00.971 --> 00:10:06.271
which reduces what a forged name can do
against Kerberos, but it doesn't change

141
00:10:06.271 --> 00:10:11.158
what the CA will sign, and other relying
parties may still trust the name.

142
00:10:11.158 --> 00:10:12.895
Clear the flag regardless.

143
00:10:12.895 --> 00:10:18.382
ESC7 is unprivileged Manage CA or
Issue and Manage Certificates.

144
00:10:18.382 --> 00:10:22.864
Build separate groups for CA
administrators, certificate managers,

145
00:10:22.864 --> 00:10:26.245
enrollment agents, auditors
and backup custodians.

146
00:10:26.245 --> 00:10:27.960
Service accounts count too:

147
00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:32.509
the Intune Certificate Connector and
lifecycle tooling both hold Issue

148
00:10:32.509 --> 00:10:36.983
and Manage Certificates, so each is a
certificate manager in all but name.

149
00:10:36.983 --> 00:10:41.408
ESC8 is an HTTP enrollment
endpoint that accepts

150
00:10:41.408 --> 00:10:45.294
NTLM without HTTPS or Extended Protection.

151
00:10:45.294 --> 00:10:50.337
Microsoft's advisory: Extended
Protection set to Required,

152
00:10:50.337 --> 00:10:55.070
Require SSL, restart IIS, and
restrict NTLM where you can.

153
00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:59.390
Better still, remove Web Enrollment
wherever nothing depends on it,

154
00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:01.760
and apply the same rules to NDES.

155
00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:06.114
Every detection in this
module depends on evidence,

156
00:11:06.114 --> 00:11:08.460
and AD CS produces none by default.

157
00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.760
Two settings are needed, and
neither works without the other.

158
00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:15.710
The CA's audit filter selects
which CA operations are audited.

159
00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:18.786
The Audit Certification
Services advanced audit

160
00:11:18.786 --> 00:11:22.447
subcategory lets the operating
system generate the events.

161
00:11:22.447 --> 00:11:27.691
Set the subcategory to Success and Failure
in a dedicated GPO linked to the CA

162
00:11:27.691 --> 00:11:31.296
servers, set the audit filter
to cover every category,

163
00:11:31.296 --> 00:11:34.573
and restart the service
in a maintenance window.

164
00:11:34.573 --> 00:11:37.994
Full auditing includes the
start and stop category,

165
00:11:37.994 --> 00:11:42.609
which can slow restarts on a CA with
a large database, so test that first.

166
00:11:42.609 --> 00:11:45.085
Then watch Microsoft's focused baseline.

167
00:11:45.085 --> 00:11:51.888
4886, 4887 and 4888 are requests received,
issued and denied: issuance volume per

168
00:11:51.888 --> 00:11:54.878
template, every credential issued,

169
00:11:54.878 --> 00:11:59.310
and probing of templates
that should be restricted.

170
00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.185
4870 is revocation evidence.

171
00:12:02.185 --> 00:12:09.510
4882 and 4890 are permission and certificate
manager changes, which is ESC7 drift.

172
00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:12.910
4885 is someone changing the audit filter.

173
00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:18.410
4892 is a CA property change,
which catches the ESC6 flag.

174
00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:22.548
4896 is rows deleted from the CA database.

175
00:12:22.548 --> 00:12:25.973
4897 is role separation enabled.

176
00:12:25.973 --> 00:12:30.570
Microsoft recommends generating each
event in a nonproduction test before

177
00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:35.023
writing the collection rule, so an
assumed event doesn't become a blind spot.

178
00:12:35.023 --> 00:12:39.097
Forward through Windows Event
Forwarding to a collector and the SIEM.

179
00:12:39.097 --> 00:12:43.400
Forwarding doesn't stop a full
local log from overwriting evidence,

180
00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:48.460
so size the CA's Security log for the
longest plausible collection outage.

181
00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:52.398
The program scores each
class for prioritization.

182
00:12:52.398 --> 00:12:54.060
Be clear about what's whose.

183
00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:57.754
The risk level, domain-compromise
and priority columns are

184
00:12:57.754 --> 00:13:01.523
the program's own scoring, carried
forward from its PKI guide.

185
00:13:01.523 --> 00:13:04.786
The detection column is from
Microsoft's documentation.

186
00:13:04.786 --> 00:13:12.710
Four classes are Critical with Immediate
priority: ESC1, ESC2, ESC6 and ESC8.

187
00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:20.872
Four are High, to be fixed within
two weeks: ESC3, ESC4, ESC5 and ESC7.

188
00:13:20.872 --> 00:13:23.097
One change from the original guide:

189
00:13:23.097 --> 00:13:27.086
ESC2 is downgraded from Yes to
Possible for domain compromise,

190
00:13:27.086 --> 00:13:31.535
because Microsoft describes its
main effect as undermining trust in

191
00:13:31.535 --> 00:13:35.985
other certificate consumers rather
than direct user impersonation.

192
00:13:35.985 --> 00:13:39.892
Immediate means the finding is
fixed, or the template or endpoint

193
00:13:39.892 --> 00:13:43.510
is taken out of service, before
the next change window closes.

194
00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:47.523
And every row is a migration
blocker until it's verified closed.

195
00:13:47.523 --> 00:13:52.910
Verified has a definition: the Defender
for Identity assessment shows no exposed

196
00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:58.139
entities, remembering that scores and
statuses update every twenty-four hours,

197
00:13:58.139 --> 00:14:02.972
plus a manual re-check for ESC5, which
Defender for Identity doesn't assess.

198
00:14:02.972 --> 00:14:04.897
That's the record the gate reads.

199
00:14:04.897 --> 00:14:10.136
Chapter 07's first wave doesn't exit
until every Critical and High finding is

200
00:14:10.136 --> 00:14:11.882
remediated and re-tested,

201
00:14:11.882 --> 00:14:16.990
and the PKI owner and the security
team have approved the assessment.

202
00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:19.952
Three baselines close the module.

203
00:14:19.952 --> 00:14:22.711
The AD CS that remains keeps running for

204
00:14:22.711 --> 00:14:26.053
months or years for BRIDGE
and RETAIN workloads.

205
00:14:26.053 --> 00:14:29.361
Remove every template
those workloads don't need

206
00:14:29.361 --> 00:14:32.266
from each CA's issuance
list; it's reversible.

207
00:14:32.266 --> 00:14:36.203
Alert on requests for templates
that should no longer be requested.

208
00:14:36.203 --> 00:14:41.602
Keep CRL publication on schedule, and test
it from representative client networks.

209
00:14:41.602 --> 00:14:44.065
Back up the CA before every change.

210
00:14:44.065 --> 00:14:47.810
Treat CA servers as Tier 0,
the program's designation,

211
00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:52.165
because the CA can mint domain
authentication credentials.

212
00:14:52.165 --> 00:14:57.749
And the program requires FIPS
140-2 or 140-3 validated modules,

213
00:14:57.749 --> 00:15:04.427
CA keys held non-exportable in an HSM,
and dual control for root ceremonies.

214
00:15:04.427 --> 00:15:09.065
Cloud PKI removes most of that
attack surface but adds its own.

215
00:15:09.065 --> 00:15:13.559
Scope administrators with custom
Intune roles and scope tags;

216
00:15:13.559 --> 00:15:17.102
there's no built-in Intune
PKI Administrator role.

217
00:15:17.102 --> 00:15:19.053
EKUs are fixed at creation.

218
00:15:19.053 --> 00:15:25.470
On Windows, set the key storage provider
to TPM, otherwise fail; a 4096-bit key

219
00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:32.302
can only live in the software provider, so
the program uses 2048-bit RSA in the TPM.

220
00:15:32.302 --> 00:15:34.514
The program sets one-year validity.

221
00:15:34.514 --> 00:15:37.290
And don't build production
CAs during a trial.

222
00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:42.915
Entra CBA is a relying party, so its
hardening is about trust and binding.

223
00:15:42.915 --> 00:15:45.765
Require high-affinity
bindings in production.

224
00:15:45.765 --> 00:15:51.023
Use a custom authentication strength
that names issuers or policy OIDs,

225
00:15:51.023 --> 00:15:54.090
up to five of each, for
sensitive resources.

226
00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:59.152
Require CRL validation; the program
wants each trusted issuing CA to

227
00:15:59.152 --> 00:16:04.303
publish a CRL at least every twenty-four
hours, because Entra caches it.

228
00:16:04.303 --> 00:16:08.761
And CA scoping, generally
available since April 2026,

229
00:16:08.761 --> 00:16:12.714
ties each CA to one group,
with up to thirty rules.

230
00:16:12.714 --> 00:16:15.970
A mistake can lock users out, so pilot it.

231
00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:19.495
Module 2: the target PKI.

232
00:16:19.495 --> 00:16:24.294
The guide this book replaces said
Microsoft Cloud PKI eliminates

233
00:16:24.294 --> 00:16:28.657
the need for on-premises AD CS
for most certificate use cases.

234
00:16:28.657 --> 00:16:31.758
That's true for devices
and wrong for the estate.

235
00:16:31.758 --> 00:16:34.833
There's no single replacement for AD CS.

236
00:16:34.833 --> 00:16:39.846
The target is four issuers, each chosen
for the subjects only it can serve:

237
00:16:39.846 --> 00:16:42.436
Cloud PKI for Intune-managed devices,

238
00:16:42.436 --> 00:16:47.366
a smaller and hardened AD CS for what
still depends on Active Directory,

239
00:16:47.366 --> 00:16:52.295
the Federal PKI for PIV, and public
CAs for names the internet must trust.

240
00:16:52.295 --> 00:16:57.183
Entra ID sits beside all of them
as a relying party, not an issuer.

241
00:16:57.183 --> 00:17:02.090
One root-of-trust decision and one
lifecycle platform hold them together.

242
00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:08.590
Cloud PKI is a Microsoft-hosted PKI
managed in the Intune admin center.

243
00:17:08.590 --> 00:17:10.901
It hosts the root and issuing CAs,

244
00:17:10.901 --> 00:17:14.497
a SCEP registration authority
for each issuing CA,

245
00:17:14.497 --> 00:17:19.890
the CRL distribution point and the AIA
endpoint, which together replace the

246
00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:25.540
on-premises CA, NDES server and Certificate
Connector for device certificates.

247
00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:26.490
What it can do.

248
00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:31.159
Issue client-authentication certificates
to Intune-enrolled Android,

249
00:17:31.159 --> 00:17:33.727
Apple and Windows devices, through SCEP.

250
00:17:33.727 --> 00:17:36.665
Publish a CRL per CA, valid seven days,

251
00:17:36.665 --> 00:17:41.853
republished every three and a half
days and on every revocation.

252
00:17:41.853 --> 00:17:44.227
And host up to three CAs per tenant.

253
00:17:44.227 --> 00:17:45.153
What it can't.

254
00:17:45.153 --> 00:17:50.992
Issue to anything not enrolled in Intune;
Intune doesn't support Windows Server,

255
00:17:50.992 --> 00:17:53.558
so no domain controller, NPS server,

256
00:17:53.558 --> 00:17:57.452
web server or SQL Server gets
a Cloud PKI certificate.

257
00:17:57.452 --> 00:18:02.066
Issue the server certificates
RADIUS and VPN gateways present;

258
00:18:02.066 --> 00:18:05.460
Microsoft says those must
come from another CA.

259
00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:06.853
Issue through PKCS.

260
00:18:06.853 --> 00:18:08.465
Answer OCSP.

261
00:18:08.465 --> 00:18:11.390
And change a CA's EKUs after creation.

262
00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:14.278
Plan the three-CA count
before you create anything.

263
00:18:14.278 --> 00:18:17.333
Keys: licensed CAs use Azure Managed HSM;

264
00:18:17.333 --> 00:18:22.427
trial CAs use software keys that
can't be converted after purchase.

265
00:18:22.427 --> 00:18:28.016
Licensing: Cloud PKI is a subscription
on top of Intune Plan 1 or 2.

266
00:18:28.016 --> 00:18:33.276
From July 2026, Microsoft 365 E5 and E7
include it for commercial customers;

267
00:18:33.276 --> 00:18:38.707
Microsoft says Government plans are
planned to align, subject to compliance

268
00:18:38.707 --> 00:18:44.053
and regulatory requirements, so confirm
the entitlement on the current SKU.

269
00:18:44.053 --> 00:18:48.184
And availability: GCC uses
the commercial Intune service,

270
00:18:48.184 --> 00:18:52.315
so availability follows it
and licensing is the question.

271
00:18:52.315 --> 00:18:58.211
Cloud PKI became available in GCC
High in the week of September 28,

272
00:18:58.211 --> 00:19:01.290
2026, and isn't supported in DoD.

273
00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:07.890
Cloud PKI offers two ways to anchor trust,
and both can coexist in one tenant.

274
00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:14.365
The Cloud PKI root CA: you create a root
and one or more issuing CAs in the cloud.

275
00:19:14.365 --> 00:19:16.240
It's a two-tier hierarchy.

276
00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:22.032
In the admin center the root's validity is
five to twenty-five years and an issuing

277
00:19:22.032 --> 00:19:27.652
CA's is two to ten, never longer than its
root; the Graph API allows custom periods.

278
00:19:27.652 --> 00:19:31.553
The catch is that every relying
party has to be given the new root.

279
00:19:31.553 --> 00:19:35.270
Bring your own CA: you create
an issuing CA in the cloud,

280
00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:40.677
Intune generates a certificate signing
request, and your private CA signs it.

281
00:19:40.677 --> 00:19:42.065
Four details matter.

282
00:19:42.065 --> 00:19:46.476
The signer doesn't have to be the root;
Microsoft says it can be a root or

283
00:19:46.476 --> 00:19:51.815
issuing CA from any tier, using the
Subordinate Certification Authority template.

284
00:19:51.815 --> 00:19:57.685
The signer sets the terms: validity and
hash algorithm come from the signing CA,

285
00:19:57.685 --> 00:20:01.929
the key size you choose caps
the SCEP profiles that use it,

286
00:20:01.929 --> 00:20:05.090
and 1024-bit keys and
SHA-1 aren't supported.

287
00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:10.135
The AIA comes from your CA, and AD
CS defaults to an LDAP location,

288
00:20:10.135 --> 00:20:13.277
so configure an HTTP AIA before signing;

289
00:20:13.277 --> 00:20:18.227
Android doesn't follow AIA paths,
so deploy the full chain there.

290
00:20:18.227 --> 00:20:21.574
And relying parties still
get one new certificate:

291
00:20:21.574 --> 00:20:26.515
Microsoft recommends deploying the
BYOCA issuing CA certificate to them,

292
00:20:26.515 --> 00:20:30.340
because AIA retrieval isn't
guaranteed on every platform.

293
00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:32.128
Renewal repeats the signing.

294
00:20:32.128 --> 00:20:35.103
One consequence matters
more than any of these.

295
00:20:35.103 --> 00:20:38.907
Under BYOCA the private
root CRL stays where it is,

296
00:20:38.907 --> 00:20:43.639
and every relying party, Entra ID
included, must still fetch it.

297
00:20:43.639 --> 00:20:48.017
An offline root that publishes
its CRL only over LDAP, or late,

298
00:20:48.017 --> 00:20:52.330
breaks the cloud issuer as
surely as the on-premises one.

299
00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:56.453
A Cloud PKI root needs
no existing private PKI,

300
00:20:56.453 --> 00:21:01.779
brings a new chain that has to reach
every relying party and every forest,

301
00:21:01.779 --> 00:21:05.330
and keeps the root key in
a Microsoft-managed HSM.

302
00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:11.373
BYOCA needs a private CA to sign, preserves
the chain relying parties already hold,

303
00:21:11.373 --> 00:21:17.416
keeps root custody wherever your AD CS root
key is, and adds one ongoing on-premises

304
00:21:17.416 --> 00:21:23.193
dependency: the private root's CRL must
stay published and reachable over HTTP.

305
00:21:23.193 --> 00:21:28.314
Neither model can serve domain
controller, LDAPS or server certificates,

306
00:21:28.314 --> 00:21:32.343
because Cloud PKI issues only
to Intune-enrolled devices.

307
00:21:32.343 --> 00:21:34.877
And rollback is simpler under BYOCA,

308
00:21:34.877 --> 00:21:39.017
because the original root and
its issuing CAs keep running.

309
00:21:39.017 --> 00:21:44.086
The program's recommendation, for
an organization with an established

310
00:21:44.086 --> 00:21:48.554
AD CS hierarchy, an offline root
and cross-forest trust, is BYOCA.

311
00:21:48.554 --> 00:21:52.772
It preserves the trust chain,
needs one cloud CA rather than two,

312
00:21:52.772 --> 00:21:55.042
and keeps a natural rollback path.

313
00:21:55.042 --> 00:22:01.123
For an organization building PKI for the
first time alongside cloud-first identity,

314
00:22:01.123 --> 00:22:05.031
a Cloud PKI root removes
every on-premises dependency.

315
00:22:05.031 --> 00:22:07.680
Three program rules go with BYOCA.

316
00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:10.920
Harden first: the root's
hierarchy passes the

317
00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:14.510
Chapter 03 gate before it
signs a cloud issuing CA.

318
00:22:14.510 --> 00:22:19.063
Sign with the root, not an AD CS
issuing CA, so the cloud issuer is a

319
00:22:19.063 --> 00:22:24.317
peer of the existing issuing CAs and
retiring them later doesn't touch it.

320
00:22:24.317 --> 00:22:26.848
And treat the root CRL as production:

321
00:22:26.848 --> 00:22:32.164
publish it to an internet-reachable HTTP
location on a schedule with margin,

322
00:22:32.164 --> 00:22:37.850
monitor it, and record the next ceremony
date before the current CRL expires.

323
00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:43.675
Chapter 04 records what AD CS is
kept for and when each use ends.

324
00:22:43.675 --> 00:22:48.929
Domain controller and KDC certificates
stay until the last domain controller

325
00:22:48.929 --> 00:22:52.193
retires: servers can't get
Cloud PKI certificates,

326
00:22:52.193 --> 00:22:56.412
and smart card or certificate
logon to the domain needs a domain

327
00:22:56.412 --> 00:23:00.950
controller certificate chaining to
a CA in the forest's NTAuth store.

328
00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:04.262
LDAPS stays until LDAP-bound
applications move.

329
00:23:04.262 --> 00:23:09.352
NPS, RADIUS and VPN gateway server
certificates stay until RADIUS moves,

330
00:23:09.352 --> 00:23:13.424
and until then they fall under
the server issuer decision.

331
00:23:13.424 --> 00:23:18.175
Network equipment through NDES and
HSM-held code signing stay too.

332
00:23:18.175 --> 00:23:22.740
And S/MIME: delivery can move
to Intune imported PKCS,

333
00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:26.138
but the issuer and its key history stay.

334
00:23:26.138 --> 00:23:28.459
That leaves internal server TLS,

335
00:23:28.459 --> 00:23:32.674
which Chapter 02 parked as BRIDGE
until an issuer was named.

336
00:23:32.674 --> 00:23:35.100
The program's decision has three parts.

337
00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:38.848
Internal names are issued by
the retained AD CS hierarchy,

338
00:23:38.848 --> 00:23:44.392
but requested and renewed through CyberArk
Certificate Manager rather than by hand

339
00:23:44.392 --> 00:23:48.921
or by server autoenrollment, so every
server certificate has an owner,

340
00:23:48.921 --> 00:23:52.200
an expiry alert and automated
renewal in one place.

341
00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:54.885
That covers NPS and RADIUS servers,

342
00:23:54.885 --> 00:24:00.163
VPN and Remote Desktop gateways and
SQL Server as well as web servers.

343
00:24:00.163 --> 00:24:05.224
Domain controller certificates, which
also serve LDAPS, stay on autoenrollment.

344
00:24:05.224 --> 00:24:09.238
Names the internet must trust come
from a public CA, also through the

345
00:24:09.238 --> 00:24:13.825
lifecycle platform, because public
lifetimes are on the shrinking schedule.

346
00:24:13.825 --> 00:24:17.472
And after Active Directory:
enterprise CAs need AD,

347
00:24:17.472 --> 00:24:20.250
and standalone CAs don't use templates.

348
00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:25.616
When the forest retires, server issuance
moves to a standalone AD CS issuing CA

349
00:24:25.616 --> 00:24:29.661
under the same root, still driven
by the lifecycle platform,

350
00:24:29.661 --> 00:24:32.550
which supports both kinds of Microsoft CA.

351
00:24:32.550 --> 00:24:38.080
That keeps server certificates on the existing
chain with no new root to distribute.

352
00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:43.687
PIV certificates aren't issued by
Cloud PKI or by the program's AD CS,

353
00:24:43.687 --> 00:24:47.043
and the migration doesn't
change their issuer.

354
00:24:47.043 --> 00:24:52.356
The Federal PKI's root for the federal
government is the Federal Common Policy CA

355
00:24:52.356 --> 00:24:55.529
G2, and shared service
providers are contracted

356
00:24:55.529 --> 00:24:58.067
to issue the certificates on PIV cards.

357
00:24:58.067 --> 00:25:02.468
What the target PKI has to do
for PIV is trust, not issue.

358
00:25:02.468 --> 00:25:06.387
Distribute the federal root,
because it isn't in the Microsoft,

359
00:25:06.387 --> 00:25:10.999
Mozilla or Apple trust stores by
default: an Intune trusted certificate

360
00:25:10.999 --> 00:25:14.918
profile for managed devices,
Group Policy for domain members.

361
00:25:14.918 --> 00:25:17.553
Keep NTAuth right while the domain exists,

362
00:25:17.553 --> 00:25:22.448
because smart card logon with certificates
from a non-Microsoft CA needs that

363
00:25:22.448 --> 00:25:27.643
issuing CA in NTAuth, and a certificate on
every authenticating domain controller.

364
00:25:27.643 --> 00:25:29.731
And upload the chain to Entra ID,

365
00:25:29.731 --> 00:25:33.667
which validates PIV certificates
against its trust store.

366
00:25:33.667 --> 00:25:34.880
Then lifecycle.

367
00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:40.417
Cloud PKI and Intune issue, renew and
revoke what they put on managed devices.

368
00:25:40.417 --> 00:25:43.451
Everything else is the
lifecycle platform's job,

369
00:25:43.451 --> 00:25:46.940
and for this program it's
CyberArk Certificate Manager,

370
00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:52.172
now part of Palo Alto Networks, from the
program's existing machine-identity vendor;

371
00:25:52.172 --> 00:25:55.281
the program doesn't add a
second lifecycle vendor.

372
00:25:55.281 --> 00:25:57.367
Two facts shape how it's deployed.

373
00:25:57.367 --> 00:26:02.465
Its CA account is privileged: to enroll
through a Microsoft CA it holds Read,

374
00:26:02.465 --> 00:26:06.410
Issue and Manage Certificates,
and Request Certificates,

375
00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:08.630
so it falls under the ESC7 review.

376
00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:13.902
And in the FedRAMP Marketplace dataset as
of October 20, 2026, none of Palo Alto

377
00:26:13.902 --> 00:26:17.115
Networks' authorized
packages is Certificate

378
00:26:17.115 --> 00:26:20.080
Manager or Next-Generation Trust Security.

379
00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:23.742
Until one is, the program
deploys Certificate Manager,

380
00:26:23.742 --> 00:26:27.242
Self-Hosted inside its own
authorization boundary.

381
00:26:27.242 --> 00:26:31.740
That's a program decision, to
revisit when the Marketplace changes.

382
00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:35.790
Module 3: device and user certificates.

383
00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:38.290
Intune puts certificates on devices.

384
00:26:38.290 --> 00:26:42.203
Entra ID decides whether a
certificate proves who someone is.

385
00:26:42.203 --> 00:26:45.502
Those are two jobs, and
each one fails differently.

386
00:26:45.502 --> 00:26:50.239
When Intune delivery fails, a device
has no certificate and Wi-Fi,

387
00:26:50.239 --> 00:26:52.652
VPN or an application refuses it.

388
00:26:52.652 --> 00:26:56.302
The failure is local and
visible in the profile report.

389
00:26:56.302 --> 00:27:01.771
When Entra certificate-based authentication
goes wrong, the danger is the opposite:

390
00:27:01.771 --> 00:27:07.004
a certificate that should fail might pass,
because the trust store holds too much,

391
00:27:07.004 --> 00:27:10.128
revocation isn't configured,
or a binding is weak.

392
00:27:10.128 --> 00:27:12.215
Keep the two jobs apart in planning.

393
00:27:12.215 --> 00:27:16.495
They meet at one point, a user
certificate delivered by Intune and then

394
00:27:16.495 --> 00:27:20.910
used to sign in, and that's where
most migration surprises come from.

395
00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:25.185
Every other profile depends on
the trusted certificate profile.

396
00:27:25.185 --> 00:27:28.433
It deploys the public
certificate of the root and of

397
00:27:28.433 --> 00:27:32.285
intermediate or issuing CAs, so
the device can build the chain.

398
00:27:32.285 --> 00:27:35.688
Export each CA certificate
as a DER-encoded file,

399
00:27:35.688 --> 00:27:39.772
never the private key, and
create one profile per platform.

400
00:27:39.772 --> 00:27:44.948
With Cloud PKI, that's the
Cloud PKI root and issuing CAs.

401
00:27:44.948 --> 00:27:48.960
With BYOCA, it's every CA
in the private hierarchy

402
00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:52.972
as well as the BYOCA issuing
CA, on every platform.

403
00:27:52.972 --> 00:27:58.373
If a device doesn't trust the root,
the SCEP or PKCS profile fails.

404
00:27:58.373 --> 00:27:59.861
Then three paths.

405
00:27:59.861 --> 00:28:05.097
SCEP with Cloud PKI: the device
generates its own key pair and request;

406
00:28:05.097 --> 00:28:09.048
the old guide said Intune
generates it, and it doesn't.

407
00:28:09.048 --> 00:28:12.842
The Cloud PKI registration
authority checks the request

408
00:28:12.842 --> 00:28:16.398
against the Intune challenge,
and the issuing CA signs.

409
00:28:16.398 --> 00:28:20.273
No on-premises CA, NDES
server or connector.

410
00:28:20.273 --> 00:28:25.698
Don't mix NDES and Cloud PKI
SCEP URLs in one profile.

411
00:28:25.698 --> 00:28:30.826
SCEP with AD CS runs through two pieces
of on-premises software: NDES as the

412
00:28:30.826 --> 00:28:34.776
registration authority, and
the Certificate Connector,

413
00:28:34.776 --> 00:28:37.298
which adds the Intune policy module.

414
00:28:37.298 --> 00:28:40.298
The private key still
never leaves the device.

415
00:28:40.298 --> 00:28:43.260
PKCS doesn't use NDES.

416
00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:46.862
The connector generates the
key pair on its own server,

417
00:28:46.862 --> 00:28:51.985
requests the certificate, and hands the
certificate and key to Intune as a PFX.

418
00:28:51.985 --> 00:28:55.785
So the template must allow export,
and the private key exists,

419
00:28:55.785 --> 00:28:59.660
however briefly, somewhere other
than the device that uses it.

420
00:28:59.660 --> 00:29:03.032
That's the trade PKCS
makes for not needing NDES,

421
00:29:03.032 --> 00:29:08.400
and it's why the program prefers SCEP
wherever a use case allows either.

422
00:29:09.150 --> 00:29:13.525
When the issuer is AD CS, the
infrastructure rules are strict.

423
00:29:13.525 --> 00:29:18.400
The CA must be an enterprise CA;
a standalone CA isn't supported.

424
00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:22.585
The NDES and connector server is
domain-joined in the same forest,

425
00:29:22.585 --> 00:29:25.995
and is neither a domain
controller nor the issuing CA.

426
00:29:25.995 --> 00:29:29.095
Publishing NDES to the
internet needs a reverse

427
00:29:29.095 --> 00:29:31.962
proxy with passthrough preauthentication.

428
00:29:31.962 --> 00:29:35.460
The SCEP template supplies
the subject in the request,

429
00:29:35.460 --> 00:29:39.346
which is exactly the setting
Chapter 03 treats as dangerous;

430
00:29:39.346 --> 00:29:42.221
here the policy module
enforces the subject,

431
00:29:42.221 --> 00:29:47.350
so the template is safe only while Enroll
is limited to the NDES service account.

432
00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:49.650
Audit that ACL like any other.

433
00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:54.395
And the connector's Issue and Manage
Certificates right is a CA officer right,

434
00:29:54.395 --> 00:29:58.325
so the connector server belongs
in the CA's administrative tier.

435
00:29:58.325 --> 00:30:01.207
NDES picks a template by
the request's purpose,

436
00:30:01.207 --> 00:30:04.013
so one server serves at
most three templates.

437
00:30:04.013 --> 00:30:08.875
It can't be clustered; for availability,
run several identical servers.

438
00:30:08.875 --> 00:30:12.279
Its own two registration-authority
certificates expire

439
00:30:12.279 --> 00:30:15.187
like any other and are a
frequent cause of outages.

440
00:30:15.187 --> 00:30:16.700
Keep the connector current.

441
00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:25.262
The current release is
6.2510.3.3007, from May 18, 2026.

442
00:30:25.262 --> 00:30:28.687
Let it update itself and
alert on Warning or Error.

443
00:30:28.687 --> 00:30:30.538
Which profile for which use.

444
00:30:30.538 --> 00:30:37.813
Wi-Fi, VPN, web application client certificates
and user certificates for Entra CBA

445
00:30:37.813 --> 00:30:40.976
are trusted plus SCEP, from Cloud PKI,

446
00:30:40.976 --> 00:30:45.405
or from AD CS while a relying
party trusts only AD CS.

447
00:30:45.405 --> 00:30:50.782
S/MIME signing is PKCS; S/MIME
encryption is imported PKCS from

448
00:30:50.782 --> 00:30:55.738
a CA with key archival, because
old mail needs the old keys.

449
00:30:55.738 --> 00:31:01.840
And a user certificate for certificate
logon to AD needs the SID added,

450
00:31:01.840 --> 00:31:04.410
from AD CS or a CA in NTAuth.

451
00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:10.635
Before May 2022, a domain controller mapped
a certificate to an account by name.

452
00:31:10.635 --> 00:31:16.435
KB5014754 changed the KDC
to want a strong mapping.

453
00:31:16.435 --> 00:31:22.073
The old guide said enforcement began in
February 2025; that was one step of four.

454
00:31:22.073 --> 00:31:24.822
May 2022, Compatibility mode.

455
00:31:24.822 --> 00:31:28.722
April 2023, the Disabled
setting is ignored.

456
00:31:28.722 --> 00:31:34.861
February 11, 2025, Full Enforcement for
domain controllers with no value set.

457
00:31:34.861 --> 00:31:40.022
September 9, 2025, the registry
key is no longer honored.

458
00:31:40.022 --> 00:31:43.581
As of October 2026, every
current domain controller

459
00:31:43.581 --> 00:31:46.973
is in Full Enforcement with
no supported way back.

460
00:31:46.973 --> 00:31:53.252
A strong mapping is the SID extension AD
CS adds, the SID in a tagged SAN URL,

461
00:31:53.252 --> 00:31:58.800
which is Intune's form, or an
explicit strong value on the account,

462
00:31:58.800 --> 00:32:03.510
such as issuer and serial
number, SKI or SHA-1 public key.

463
00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:05.448
What Intune profiles need.

464
00:32:05.448 --> 00:32:10.805
It applies to user certificates on all
platforms and to device certificates only

465
00:32:10.805 --> 00:32:15.847
on hybrid joined Windows devices, and the
user or device must be synced from AD.

466
00:32:15.847 --> 00:32:19.189
For SCEP, add a SAN
attribute of type URI with

467
00:32:19.189 --> 00:32:22.810
the on-premises security
identifier variable.

468
00:32:22.810 --> 00:32:26.093
The old guide said Cloud
PKI adds it automatically;

469
00:32:26.093 --> 00:32:28.985
Microsoft's instructions
say you must add it.

470
00:32:28.985 --> 00:32:32.282
For PKCS, run the
current connector and set

471
00:32:32.282 --> 00:32:35.673
the SID security extension registry value.

472
00:32:35.673 --> 00:32:39.760
Logon certificates need
their issuing CA in NTAuth.

473
00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:45.035
Pilot with a new profile, because editing
a SCEP profile revokes what it issued.

474
00:32:45.035 --> 00:32:51.748
To find weak certificates, sweep the
KDC's System log for events 39, 40 and 41.

475
00:32:51.748 --> 00:32:56.608
One trap on a mixed estate:
on Server 2008 and 2008 R2,

476
00:32:56.608 --> 00:33:03.436
event 41 means no strong mapping, and
the other two are logged as 48 and 49.

477
00:33:03.436 --> 00:33:06.780
Read the event text before
you classify a hit.

478
00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:11.180
How a CBA sign-in works,
as Microsoft documents it.

479
00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:15.020
The user enters a username;
Entra ID performs home realm

480
00:33:15.020 --> 00:33:18.693
discovery and offers Use a
certificate or smart card.

481
00:33:18.693 --> 00:33:23.075
The client is redirected to the
certificate authentication endpoint,

482
00:33:23.075 --> 00:33:27.531
certauth dot login dot microsoftonline
dot com in the commercial cloud,

483
00:33:27.531 --> 00:33:31.393
which includes GCC, and the dot
us endpoint in Azure Government.

484
00:33:31.393 --> 00:33:35.355
It requests the client certificate
in a mutual-TLS handshake.

485
00:33:35.355 --> 00:33:37.906
Entra ID checks the chain and the CRL,

486
00:33:37.906 --> 00:33:41.392
and finds one user through
the username bindings.

487
00:33:41.392 --> 00:33:47.070
If Conditional Access requires MFA and
the certificate counts as multifactor, the

488
00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:52.418
user is signed in; if it's single-factor,
Entra offers a registered second factor.

489
00:33:52.418 --> 00:33:56.753
Allow the endpoint through proxies
and turn off TLS inspection for it,

490
00:33:56.753 --> 00:33:59.818
or the client certificate
never reaches Entra ID.

491
00:33:59.818 --> 00:34:00.954
The trust store.

492
00:34:00.954 --> 00:34:04.980
Upload every CA in the chain
that issues user certificates.

493
00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:08.725
Microsoft highly recommends
the PKI-based trust store,

494
00:34:08.725 --> 00:34:11.330
which holds up to two hundred fifty CAs.

495
00:34:11.330 --> 00:34:15.606
Managing it takes Privileged
Authentication Administrator,

496
00:34:15.606 --> 00:34:17.580
and bulk upload needs P1 or P2.

497
00:34:17.580 --> 00:34:21.485
The trust store is also a blast
radius: adding a CA lets any

498
00:34:21.485 --> 00:34:25.805
certificate it issues authenticate
any user it can be bound to.

499
00:34:25.805 --> 00:34:26.817
Revocation.

500
00:34:26.817 --> 00:34:31.790
Set each CA's base CRL URL, and the
delta if published; without one,

501
00:34:31.790 --> 00:34:38.006
a revoked certificate still authenticates,
so turn on Require CRL validation.

502
00:34:38.006 --> 00:34:43.524
Entra ID supports one CRL endpoint
per CA over HTTP or HTTPS,

503
00:34:43.524 --> 00:34:48.593
with HTTP recommended,
downloaded within ten seconds.

504
00:34:48.593 --> 00:34:50.817
It doesn't support OCSP.

505
00:34:50.817 --> 00:34:55.132
Interactive sign-in downloads CRLs
up to twenty megabytes in the

506
00:34:55.132 --> 00:34:59.605
commercial cloud including GCC,
and forty-five in Azure Government.

507
00:34:59.605 --> 00:35:02.494
And it caches the CRL
until its next update,

508
00:35:02.494 --> 00:35:07.230
so revoke the user's sessions when
revocation must be immediate.

509
00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:12.318
Username bindings map a certificate
field to a user attribute.

510
00:35:12.318 --> 00:35:16.831
By default the SAN principal name
maps to the user principal name.

511
00:35:16.831 --> 00:35:21.104
Microsoft classes subject key
identifier, SHA-1 public key,

512
00:35:21.104 --> 00:35:24.419
and issuer and serial
number as high affinity,

513
00:35:24.419 --> 00:35:28.955
and principal name, email, subject,
and issuer and subject as low.

514
00:35:28.955 --> 00:35:33.896
The old guide classed issuer plus subject
as high; Microsoft classes it as low,

515
00:35:33.896 --> 00:35:38.143
because a CA can issue several
certificates with the same subject.

516
00:35:38.143 --> 00:35:40.317
How Entra ID evaluates them.

517
00:35:40.317 --> 00:35:45.555
In priority order, lowest number first,
and only Graph shows the priority.

518
00:35:45.555 --> 00:35:49.130
If the tenant or a custom
rule requires high affinity,

519
00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:51.730
every low-affinity binding is dropped.

520
00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:56.855
So with several bindings, CBA is only
as secure as the lowest-affinity one.

521
00:35:56.855 --> 00:35:59.780
Configure one low-affinity
binding at most.

522
00:35:59.780 --> 00:36:04.333
The program's position: production
tenants require high affinity,

523
00:36:04.333 --> 00:36:08.555
using issuer and serial number
or SKI in certificate user IDs.

524
00:36:08.555 --> 00:36:12.430
Low-affinity UPN binding
is acceptable in a pilot.

525
00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:15.682
But setting the tenant-wide
requirement to high before

526
00:36:15.682 --> 00:36:18.792
every user's values are
correct locks those users out.

527
00:36:18.792 --> 00:36:22.867
The authentication binding policy
decides one factor or two.

528
00:36:22.867 --> 00:36:28.706
The default is single-factor; rules can
be by issuer, by policy OID, or both.

529
00:36:28.706 --> 00:36:33.962
Issuer-and-OID rules beat OID rules,
and OID rules beat issuer rules;

530
00:36:33.962 --> 00:36:38.168
a certificate whose OIDs map
to both is single-factor.

531
00:36:38.168 --> 00:36:43.450
Microsoft says to treat a certificate as
multifactor only if a PIN or biometric

532
00:36:43.450 --> 00:36:48.092
protects it, so a PIV card qualifies
and a software certificate doesn't.

533
00:36:48.092 --> 00:36:49.830
Two rules prevent lockout.

534
00:36:49.830 --> 00:36:54.160
Target CBA to groups that hold
valid certificates, never All users,

535
00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:59.592
and give people without a certificate another
path, such as a Temporary Access Pass.

536
00:36:59.592 --> 00:37:03.955
CBA itself needs no premium
license; Conditional Access,

537
00:37:03.955 --> 00:37:07.560
which every real
deployment uses, needs P1.

538
00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:13.897
Staged rollout lets synchronized users
in a federated domain sign in at Entra

539
00:37:13.897 --> 00:37:19.398
ID with CBA instead of being sent to AD
FS, while everyone else stays federated.

540
00:37:19.398 --> 00:37:23.127
Microsoft is explicit that it's
a temporary testing mechanism,

541
00:37:23.127 --> 00:37:27.934
not a permanent mixed state, and its limits
are tighter than the old guide implied.

542
00:37:27.934 --> 00:37:32.417
It applies only to users synced by
Entra Connect, and only to browser and

543
00:37:32.417 --> 00:37:37.735
modern authentication; legacy authentication
still goes to the federation server.

544
00:37:37.735 --> 00:37:41.697
Up to ten groups per feature,
and no nested or dynamic groups.

545
00:37:41.697 --> 00:37:45.384
A group added for the first time
is limited to two hundred users,

546
00:37:45.384 --> 00:37:48.786
and later membership changes
take up to twenty-four hours.

547
00:37:48.786 --> 00:37:53.735
Password sign-in for CBA pilot users
works only with password hash sync.

548
00:37:53.735 --> 00:37:57.526
And issue a Temporary Access
Pass right after adding a user,

549
00:37:57.526 --> 00:38:01.472
so the first sign-in doesn't fall
back to the federation server.

550
00:38:01.472 --> 00:38:05.155
The program pilots with cloud
groups of ten to twenty users,

551
00:38:05.155 --> 00:38:09.891
confirms in the sign-in log which binding
matched and what level was granted,

552
00:38:09.891 --> 00:38:12.672
and grows in steps until
the domain converts.

553
00:38:12.672 --> 00:38:17.716
Moving off AD FS certificate authentication
doesn't change the credential or

554
00:38:17.716 --> 00:38:22.760
the PKI; only the place where authentication
and revocation are checked moves.

555
00:38:22.760 --> 00:38:26.133
Get trust parity, with
internet-reachable CRLs.

556
00:38:26.133 --> 00:38:30.544
Get binding parity, syncing
account mappings to certificate

557
00:38:30.544 --> 00:38:34.522
user IDs where AD FS used them,
and protect that sync path.

558
00:38:34.522 --> 00:38:38.354
Keep staged rollout short,
because staged users stay exposed

559
00:38:38.354 --> 00:38:42.185
to a federation server compromise
until the domain converts.

560
00:38:42.185 --> 00:38:45.710
Record the federation
configuration and back up the farm.

561
00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:49.172
And the old guide called
conversion one-way; it isn't.

562
00:38:49.172 --> 00:38:51.804
Microsoft's rollback is to re-federate,

563
00:38:51.804 --> 00:38:55.790
which works only while AD FS
can still be brought back.

564
00:38:56.540 --> 00:39:00.740
Module 4: coexistence,
migration and retirement.

565
00:39:00.740 --> 00:39:03.922
Coexistence isn't a phase
at the end of the project;

566
00:39:03.922 --> 00:39:06.328
it's the condition the project runs in.

567
00:39:06.328 --> 00:39:11.055
From the day the first Cloud PKI
certificate is issued until the last AD

568
00:39:11.055 --> 00:39:16.263
CS-dependent workload retires, devices
carry certificates from both issuers,

569
00:39:16.263 --> 00:39:21.390
relying parties trust both chains, and
two sets of CRLs have to stay reachable.

570
00:39:21.390 --> 00:39:24.903
For most of the migration,
both PKIs are production.

571
00:39:24.903 --> 00:39:27.773
The plan has to say which
one each certificate

572
00:39:27.773 --> 00:39:30.491
comes from on any given
day, and how to go back.

573
00:39:30.491 --> 00:39:35.609
Then, after the migration, the work
becomes keeping every certificate owned,

574
00:39:35.609 --> 00:39:41.240
renewed and checkable, and retiring the
old CAs without stranding a single one.

575
00:39:41.990 --> 00:39:44.153
The program's coexistence rules.

576
00:39:44.153 --> 00:39:49.371
Each use case has one issuer at a time
per population: a device group gets

577
00:39:49.371 --> 00:39:54.414
its Wi-Fi certificate from AD CS or
from Cloud PKI, never both by design.

578
00:39:54.414 --> 00:39:58.862
Trust goes up before issuance moves:
a relying party trusts the new chain

579
00:39:58.862 --> 00:40:03.753
before the first certificate from it
reaches anything that relying party sees.

580
00:40:03.753 --> 00:40:07.552
Revocation must be reachable
wherever validation happens.

581
00:40:07.552 --> 00:40:10.022
Nothing is revoked to force a move:

582
00:40:10.022 --> 00:40:13.903
an AD CS certificate is
replaced at renewal or expiry.

583
00:40:13.903 --> 00:40:16.153
And every move has a tested rollback that

584
00:40:16.153 --> 00:40:18.602
doesn't depend on
something the move retired.

585
00:40:18.602 --> 00:40:22.340
What stays on AD CS until
Active Directory retires.

586
00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:27.478
Domain controller certificates, because
Cloud PKI doesn't issue to servers.

587
00:40:27.478 --> 00:40:30.528
Use a copy of the Kerberos
Authentication template.

588
00:40:30.528 --> 00:40:35.977
Entra-joined devices can't read a
CRL from AD before they authenticate,

589
00:40:35.977 --> 00:40:38.702
so publish an HTTP CRL listed first.

590
00:40:38.702 --> 00:40:42.926
LDAPS stays too; the old guide
said it could move to Cloud PKI,

591
00:40:42.926 --> 00:40:45.253
but an LDAPS endpoint is a server.

592
00:40:45.253 --> 00:40:48.579
Other server certificates
stay on AD CS as well,

593
00:40:48.579 --> 00:40:53.527
renewed through CyberArk Certificate
Manager, as Chapter 04 decided.

594
00:40:53.527 --> 00:40:59.696
NPS needs care: it must trust the Cloud PKI
chain before the first pilot device gets

595
00:40:59.696 --> 00:41:03.143
a cloud Wi-Fi certificate,
and keep trusting AD

596
00:41:03.143 --> 00:41:06.953
CS until the last AD CS
client certificate expires.

597
00:41:06.953 --> 00:41:08.628
Trust runs both ways.

598
00:41:08.628 --> 00:41:14.078
AD CS into Entra ID: upload the root
and every intermediate and issuing CA,

599
00:41:14.078 --> 00:41:18.257
each with an internet-facing
CRL; that's also what lets AD

600
00:41:18.257 --> 00:41:23.253
FS certificate authentication
move without reissuing anything.

601
00:41:23.253 --> 00:41:28.527
The cloud chain into the forest: by Group
Policy or by publishing to the directory.

602
00:41:28.527 --> 00:41:32.553
NTAuth only for CAs that will
issue logon certificates.

603
00:41:32.553 --> 00:41:35.360
And each forest needs
its own distribution,

604
00:41:35.360 --> 00:41:40.700
while every forest's user-issuing root
goes into the single Entra trust store.

605
00:41:41.450 --> 00:41:44.713
Most coexistence outages
are revocation outages.

606
00:41:44.713 --> 00:41:49.981
Relying parties now sit in three places,
inside the network, on the internet,

607
00:41:49.981 --> 00:41:53.380
and in Entra ID, and each
CA's CRL must be reachable

608
00:41:53.380 --> 00:41:57.374
from all three wherever its
certificates are validated.

609
00:41:57.374 --> 00:42:04.025
Every relying party that validates Cloud
PKI certificates, including on-premises

610
00:42:04.025 --> 00:42:10.000
RADIUS and VPN servers, needs outbound
HTTP to the Intune-hosted CDP and AIA.

611
00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:16.140
A BYOCA issuing CA's own CRL is
Intune-hosted too, but its parent CRL and

612
00:42:16.140 --> 00:42:22.387
AIA come from the private CA, and AD
CS defaults to an LDAP AIA location.

613
00:42:22.387 --> 00:42:29.606
AD CS CAs that issue user certificates for
Entra CBA usually publish over LDAP plus

614
00:42:29.606 --> 00:42:35.690
an internal HTTP URL; Entra ID needs
one internet HTTP or HTTPS URL per CA,

615
00:42:35.690 --> 00:42:41.775
HTTP recommended, with no OCSP and no
LDAP, and watch the CRL size limits,

616
00:42:41.775 --> 00:42:48.375
twenty megabytes for commercial and GCC
and forty-five for Azure Government.

617
00:42:48.375 --> 00:42:53.338
And the CAs behind domain controller
certificates must serve Entra-joined devices

618
00:42:53.338 --> 00:42:57.875
that haven't authenticated yet, so
LDAP-only distribution points break them.

619
00:42:57.875 --> 00:43:01.850
Fixing an AD CS distribution
point means two things.

620
00:43:01.850 --> 00:43:08.725
Add an HTTP location reachable from the
internet, using HTTP rather than HTTPS.

621
00:43:08.725 --> 00:43:14.250
And recognize that the new URL appears only
in certificates issued after the change.

622
00:43:14.250 --> 00:43:17.946
Entra ID uses the CRL URL
configured in its trust store,

623
00:43:17.946 --> 00:43:20.409
not the one inside each certificate,

624
00:43:20.409 --> 00:43:24.844
so CBA works as soon as the trust
store points at the published CRL;

625
00:43:24.844 --> 00:43:29.525
other relying parties keep failing
until their certificates renew.

626
00:43:29.525 --> 00:43:35.166
The program publishes on plain anonymous
HTTP, not through an application proxy,

627
00:43:35.166 --> 00:43:41.340
because Entra ID must download the CRL
without authentication within ten seconds.

628
00:43:42.090 --> 00:43:44.365
The migration runs as six waves.

629
00:43:44.365 --> 00:43:49.378
Each wave's exit gate is the next one's
entry gate, and each has its own rollback.

630
00:43:49.378 --> 00:43:53.971
The durations are program planning
figures for a single-forest estate,

631
00:43:53.971 --> 00:43:57.240
not commitments; scale them
to the wave 1 inventory.

632
00:43:57.240 --> 00:44:00.165
Appendix A turns each
wave into a checklist

633
00:44:00.165 --> 00:44:02.928
with an owner and a verification per line.

634
00:44:02.928 --> 00:44:08.086
Wave 1, Assess, weeks one to four:
inventory every CA and template,

635
00:44:08.086 --> 00:44:12.551
run the Chapter 03 checklist,
classify every template,

636
00:44:12.551 --> 00:44:17.314
list every relying party, and
run the strong-mapping sweep.

637
00:44:17.314 --> 00:44:19.802
It reads, so it needs no rollback.

638
00:44:19.802 --> 00:44:24.420
Wave 2, Foundation: create the
Cloud PKI CAs with licensed keys,

639
00:44:24.420 --> 00:44:29.037
upload user-issuing CAs to the
Entra trust store, deploy trust

640
00:44:29.037 --> 00:44:34.378
profiles and the cloud chain to
domain-joined relying parties, and test.

641
00:44:34.378 --> 00:44:39.051
The gate is trust profiles on at
least ninety-nine percent of managed

642
00:44:39.051 --> 00:44:43.315
devices and every CRL reachable
from inside, outside and Entra.

643
00:44:43.315 --> 00:44:49.291
Wave 3, Pilot, fifty to a hundred
devices: Wi-Fi then VPN, then CBA through

644
00:44:49.291 --> 00:44:55.477
staged rollout, with AD CS autoenrollment
left on so the rollback works.

645
00:44:55.477 --> 00:44:58.715
The gate is ninety-five
percent issuance success.

646
00:44:58.715 --> 00:45:02.179
Wave 4, Scale, in rings;
remove autoenrollment for

647
00:45:02.179 --> 00:45:05.903
each ring once its cloud
certificates are accepted.

648
00:45:05.903 --> 00:45:08.784
Wave 5, Cutover: record
federation settings,

649
00:45:08.784 --> 00:45:13.666
convert domains starting with the
smallest, and keep AD FS restorable for

650
00:45:13.666 --> 00:45:18.789
thirty days, which is what makes the
four-hour recovery objective achievable.

651
00:45:18.789 --> 00:45:20.627
Wave 6, Decommission.

652
00:45:20.627 --> 00:45:25.071
The rules across waves: rehearse
each rollback in a test scope first,

653
00:45:25.071 --> 00:45:29.515
and never let a rollback depend on
a component the same wave retires.

654
00:45:29.515 --> 00:45:34.220
Removing or editing a SCEP profile
revokes its certificates on Windows

655
00:45:34.220 --> 00:45:39.050
and Apple platforms, so treat profile
changes as changes with impact.

656
00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:43.525
Appendix B is the starting
matrix that seeds wave 1.

657
00:45:43.525 --> 00:45:47.550
Classify each template by what it
actually issued, not by its name.

658
00:45:47.550 --> 00:45:49.612
Three facts drive most rows.

659
00:45:49.612 --> 00:45:54.666
Cloud PKI issues only through SCEP,
only to Intune-enrolled devices,

660
00:45:54.666 --> 00:45:57.733
and Intune doesn't manage Windows Server,

661
00:45:57.733 --> 00:46:02.425
so every server certificate is
BRIDGE or RETAIN, never MIGRATE.

662
00:46:02.425 --> 00:46:08.600
And PKCS profiles move delivery to
Intune, not issuance off AD CS.

663
00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:14.842
So: User, Computer and Workstation
Authentication are MIGRATE for Intune-managed

664
00:46:14.842 --> 00:46:18.698
devices, to Cloud PKI SCEP,
with the SID URI where the

665
00:46:18.698 --> 00:46:23.563
certificate is used against AD;
servers stay on autoenrollment.

666
00:46:23.563 --> 00:46:28.337
Web Server is RETAIN, on AD CS or a
public CA for internet-facing names,

667
00:46:28.337 --> 00:46:33.112
with lifecycle automation through
CyberArk Certificate Manager.

668
00:46:33.112 --> 00:46:38.987
The old guide had it as MIGRATE to Cloud
PKI; web servers aren't Intune devices.

669
00:46:38.987 --> 00:46:43.933
Kerberos Authentication and LDAPS are BRIDGE
until the last domain controller; the

670
00:46:43.933 --> 00:46:48.950
domain controller certificate from the
Kerberos Authentication copy serves LDAPS.

671
00:46:48.950 --> 00:46:53.541
The older Domain Controller templates
lack the KDC Authentication OID,

672
00:46:53.541 --> 00:46:57.138
so they're superseded by a
Kerberos Authentication copy.

673
00:46:57.138 --> 00:46:59.827
RAS and IAS Server stays while NPS runs,

674
00:46:59.827 --> 00:47:03.188
renewed through CyberArk
Certificate Manager.

675
00:47:03.188 --> 00:47:06.088
Exchange Signature Only migrates delivery.

676
00:47:06.088 --> 00:47:11.596
Exchange User carries two verdicts: MIGRATE
for delivery, because Intune delivers

677
00:47:11.596 --> 00:47:16.625
it, and RETAIN for the issuer, because
the existing CA and its key history stay.

678
00:47:16.625 --> 00:47:20.319
EFS retires, but the
recovery agent retires last:

679
00:47:20.319 --> 00:47:25.213
keep its certificates and keys
until no encrypted data remains.

680
00:47:25.213 --> 00:47:29.122
Before any MIGRATE row moves,
four things must be true,

681
00:47:29.122 --> 00:47:31.788
and they're wave 4's per-ring exit test.

682
00:47:31.788 --> 00:47:36.137
Every relying party trusts the
new chain and can reach its CRL.

683
00:47:36.137 --> 00:47:39.926
The Intune profile issues at
the program's success threshold.

684
00:47:39.926 --> 00:47:44.037
Certificates used against Active
Directory carry a strong mapping.

685
00:47:44.037 --> 00:47:48.377
And the template's remaining certificates
are on a dated path to expiry,

686
00:47:48.377 --> 00:47:52.275
with autoenrollment removed only
after the profile is issuing.

687
00:47:52.275 --> 00:47:55.206
For BRIDGE rows, record
the event that ends them,

688
00:47:55.206 --> 00:47:58.720
so retirement is scheduled
rather than forgotten.

689
00:47:59.470 --> 00:48:02.745
Migration ends on a
date; certificates don't.

690
00:48:02.745 --> 00:48:08.607
Public TLS validity falls to
forty-seven days from March 15, 2029.

691
00:48:08.607 --> 00:48:11.937
The schedule binds publicly
trusted certificates,

692
00:48:11.937 --> 00:48:17.046
but the program aligns internal lifetimes
with it as automation proves itself.

693
00:48:17.046 --> 00:48:21.970
The design rule: no certificate class may
depend on a person remembering a date.

694
00:48:21.970 --> 00:48:24.420
The target has three renewal engines.

695
00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:28.705
Intune profiles renew certificates
on managed devices at the

696
00:48:28.705 --> 00:48:32.495
profile's renewal threshold,
with a new key pair for SCEP.

697
00:48:32.495 --> 00:48:37.504
On Apple platforms, renewal happens only
inside the threshold window with the device

698
00:48:37.504 --> 00:48:39.902
unlocked, and a failed renewal leaves the

699
00:48:39.902 --> 00:48:42.583
expired certificate in
place without a retry.

700
00:48:42.583 --> 00:48:47.007
AD CS autoenrollment renews
domain controller certificates,

701
00:48:47.007 --> 00:48:51.771
which also serve LDAPS, so the CA
must stay online and its CRL current.

702
00:48:51.771 --> 00:48:57.195
And CyberArk Certificate Manager
discovers, tracks and renews server TLS,

703
00:48:57.195 --> 00:49:00.520
including NPS and RADIUS
servers, appliances,

704
00:49:00.520 --> 00:49:05.770
public TLS and network devices, with
a Server Owner on every certificate.

705
00:49:05.770 --> 00:49:08.849
Neither Microsoft issuer
in the target offers ACME,

706
00:49:08.849 --> 00:49:12.358
so the program provides it
through the lifecycle platform.

707
00:49:12.358 --> 00:49:18.733
CyberArk documents the
http-01 and dns-01 challenges.

708
00:49:18.733 --> 00:49:24.560
One federal limit: CyberArk's installation
guide says FIPS mode isn't supported for

709
00:49:24.560 --> 00:49:27.938
its ACME service at all,
so run the ACME service on

710
00:49:27.938 --> 00:49:31.570
dedicated servers outside
the FIPS-mode server set.

711
00:49:31.570 --> 00:49:36.193
The drift checks run at least weekly and
are committed to the evidence repository,

712
00:49:36.193 --> 00:49:39.320
because an assessor asks how
long a finding stayed open.

713
00:49:39.320 --> 00:49:44.430
Watch for certificates within two renewal
windows of expiry with no engine bound,

714
00:49:44.430 --> 00:49:47.511
issuance outside policy,
missing strong mapping,

715
00:49:47.511 --> 00:49:52.320
trust store anchors that aren't approved,
stale CRLs, and posture regressions.

716
00:49:52.320 --> 00:49:55.213
And treat a SID mismatch
as a security event:

717
00:49:55.213 --> 00:49:59.070
it can mean the certificate
was issued to a different user.

718
00:49:59.070 --> 00:50:03.261
That's event 41 on current
domain controllers,

719
00:50:03.261 --> 00:50:10.020
but event 49 on Server 2008 and 2008
R2, where 41 means no strong mapping.

720
00:50:10.770 --> 00:50:15.332
Last, retiring AD CS, which
is the program's sixth wave.

721
00:50:15.332 --> 00:50:20.622
It applies only to the CAs and role services
that no longer serve a retained class;

722
00:50:20.622 --> 00:50:24.964
domain controller, LDAPS and RADIUS
certificates usually keep at

723
00:50:24.964 --> 00:50:29.307
least one issuing CA alive for as
long as domain controllers exist.

724
00:50:29.307 --> 00:50:34.504
The gate: cutover complete, the thirty-day
rollback window passed with no incidents,

725
00:50:34.504 --> 00:50:37.969
and every retained class
running on the CAs that remain.

726
00:50:37.969 --> 00:50:40.285
Confirm nothing depends on the CA,

727
00:50:40.285 --> 00:50:43.757
using the lifecycle inventory
and the CA database.

728
00:50:43.757 --> 00:50:46.629
Stop new issuance: remove the templates,

729
00:50:46.629 --> 00:50:50.021
unlink autoenrollment,
deny pending requests.

730
00:50:50.021 --> 00:50:51.635
Back up the database and key,

731
00:50:51.635 --> 00:50:55.145
with the backup and its password
through separate channels.

732
00:50:55.145 --> 00:50:58.977
Revoke outstanding certificates
for cessation of operation,

733
00:50:58.977 --> 00:51:03.620
set the CRL lifetime past the remaining
certificates' lifetime, and publish.

734
00:51:03.620 --> 00:51:07.536
Remove NDES and the web
services before the CA,

735
00:51:07.536 --> 00:51:11.245
and move any OCSP
consumers to CRL checking.

736
00:51:11.245 --> 00:51:17.745
Clean up trust in Entra, Intune, Group
Policy, NTAuth and the lifecycle platform.

737
00:51:17.745 --> 00:51:23.368
Keep the CDP and AIA endpoints serving
until the last certificate expires, delete

738
00:51:23.368 --> 00:51:26.842
templates only after every
CA in the forest is gone,

739
00:51:26.842 --> 00:51:29.157
and commit a decommission record.

740
00:51:29.157 --> 00:51:35.371
Under BYOCA, the root and any CA that
signed the Cloud PKI issuing CA stay.

741
00:51:35.371 --> 00:51:41.098
The assessor reads NIST
SP 800-53 evidence:

742
00:51:41.098 --> 00:51:45.141
key storage and binding settings
for IA-5 enhancement two,

743
00:51:45.141 --> 00:51:50.616
the trust store and CRL configuration
for path validation, HSM key protection

744
00:51:50.616 --> 00:51:55.670
for SC-12, and the certificate policy
and approved trust anchors for SC-17.

745
00:51:55.670 --> 00:52:01.184
So, from Monday: harden first and gate
on it, choose the root deliberately,

746
00:52:01.184 --> 00:52:06.698
issue strongly mapped certificates,
run both PKIs with reachable CRLs,

747
00:52:06.698 --> 00:52:09.408
and retire AD CS last, with evidence.

748
00:52:09.408 --> 00:52:14.158
Chapters 03 through 08 and the
appendices carry the detail.

749
00:52:14.158 --> 00:52:14.970
Thank you.
